# 23. Important Architectural Constraints

Do not:

* Hardcode CoolProp into the simulation engine
* Add thermodynamic-specific columns to the core mote schema
* Make turbine rules directly import CoolProp
* Store Python class names in the database
* Assume every property model supports the same operations
* Assume all thermodynamic properties use `(P,T)` as inputs
* Build a giant universal scientific-data schema before a real use case requires it
* Couple the property system directly to SQLite
* Make the first implementation unnecessarily generic
* Give a rule's `equation` string any way to call Python directly -- the
  `_call()` whitelist in `core/formula.py` is a closed, hardcoded list;
  add exactly one generic `property(...)` entry to it rather than one
  entry per capability
* Resolve providers (walk `uses_model`, construct a CoolProp state)
  from inside a formula evaluation -- it runs many times per run
  (substitution passes, time-series steps); resolve once per run instead
* Add `uses_model` (or `provider`/`configuration`) without registering
  them in `core/reserved.py`

Do:

* Treat models as first-class motes
* Use relationships to attach models to physical entities
* Keep external libraries behind adapters
* Represent capabilities explicitly
* Keep provider configuration data-driven
* Keep simulation rules provider-agnostic
* Keep the database representation separate from executable Python objects
* Make the system extensible without making the first version enormous
* Keep `core/formula.py` free of database access -- inject a
  pre-resolved model map as a plain callable on `Context`, same as
  `targets`/`sources`/`properties` already are
* Have a property-model call raise `NonlinearError` (not a fatal
  `FormulaError`) when its inputs aren't fully known yet, so the
  substitution loop can retry it

